---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, STUDIO } from '../../site';

const effective = 'November 12, 2026';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Shelly Jigsaw privacy policy"
  description="How the Shelly Jigsaw app by Ohana Studios handles your information, including cloud saves with Google Play Games and iCloud, game measurement with Google Analytics for Firebase, ad measurement with Meta, and ads from Google AdMob."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="card prose-card">
      <header class="page-head">
        <h1>Shelly Jigsaw privacy policy</h1>
        <p>Effective {effective}</p>
      </header>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          This policy explains what information the <strong>Shelly Jigsaw</strong> app for Android
          and iPhone (<code>com.ohanastudios.shellyjigsaw</code>) uses, why, and the choices you
          have.
          The app is made by {STUDIO} ("we", "us").
        </p>

        <h2>The short version</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>You do not create an account, and we never ask for your name or email.</li>
          <li>
            If you turn one of your own photos into a puzzle, it stays on your phone. It is never
            uploaded or backed up, and we never see it. When you finish a puzzle, you can tap
            Share to send or save the framed picture using your phone's share sheet.
          </li>
          <li>
            Your puzzle progress and settings are saved on your phone. If you use cloud saving, a
            backup copy is kept in your own account (Google Play Games on Android, iCloud on iPhone)
            so you can get it back on a new phone. We never receive your progress.
          </li>
          <li>
            We use <strong>Google Analytics for Firebase</strong> to count, in general, how the game
            is played, such as how many puzzles are started and finished. It never receives your
            name, email or photos.
          </li>
          <li>
            The game is free and shows ads from <strong>Google AdMob</strong>. Google collects some
            device information to show and measure those ads, as described below.
          </li>
          <li>
            We advertise the game on Facebook and Instagram. In many countries the Android app tells
            <strong>Meta</strong> when it is installed and opened and when you finish your first
            puzzle, so we can see whether those ads work. It never sends your name, email or photos.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Information stored on your phone</h2>
        <p>
          The app saves your puzzle progress, finished puzzles, stars and shells, Shelly's
          field notes, settings (such as sound and vibration), simple counters that decide how
          often ads appear and, if you turn on the Daily reminder, the hour you usually first open
          the game each day. This information is kept in the app's private storage on your device and
          is never sent to us. Uninstalling the app, or clearing its data in Android settings,
          deletes it from your phone. On iPhone, deleting the app deletes it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="your-own-photos">Your own photos</h2>
        <p>
          You can choose a photo from your phone to play as a puzzle. You pick it in your phone's own
          photo picker, so the app only receives the one photo you choose and never asks for access
          to your photo library. The app keeps a copy, cropped to the puzzle's shape, in its private
          storage on your phone, and deletes it when no unfinished puzzle uses it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Your photos are never sent to us, to Google Analytics, or to your cloud save, and nobody
          else sees them unless you choose to share a finished puzzle with the
          <strong>Share</strong> button. When you share, the app passes the framed picture directly
          to your phone's share sheet, where you decide where to send or save it. No ads appear
          next to a puzzle made from your photo.
        </p>
        <p>
          You are responsible for the photos you choose. Please only use photos you own or have
          permission to use.
        </p>

        <h2>Cloud save with Google Play Games (Android)</h2>
        <p>
          So you do not lose your progress when you change phones or reinstall the game, Shelly
          Jigsaw backs up your progress with <strong>Google Play Games</strong>. When the game
          starts, Google Play Games signs you in with your Google account. You can choose
          <strong>Not now</strong> on Google's sign-in message, and the game then keeps your progress
          on your phone only.
        </p>
        <p>When you are signed in:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            A copy of your game progress (puzzle progress, finished puzzles, stars and shells,
            Shelly's field notes and game settings) is saved in a private game folder in your own
            Google account. Only Shelly Jigsaw can read it, and only for your account. It does not
            include your photos, contacts, or the ad counters.
          </li>
          <li>
            The game receives your Play Games player ID, a code for your Play Games profile, so it
            can make sure a backup is restored to the same account. It does not receive your email
            address, and we do not keep the ID anywhere except on your phone.
          </li>
          <li>
            When you install the game on a new phone, or reinstall it, your saved progress is
            restored. If both your phone and your backup have progress, the game asks which one to
            keep.
          </li>
          <li>
            The game also unlocks Shelly Jigsaw achievements in Google Play Games, based on the
            puzzles you finish. Google Play Games keeps them with your Play Games profile.
          </li>
          <li>
            Google Play Games itself collects some information, such as your Play Games profile and
            information to keep its service working, as described in the
            <a href="https://developer.android.com/games/pgs/data-collection" rel="noopener">Play Games Services data disclosure</a>
            and the <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener">Google Privacy Policy</a>.
            This data is sent to Google over an encrypted connection, not to us.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <strong>Your choices.</strong> You can turn cloud saving off at any time with
          <strong>Settings &gt; Cloud save &gt; Turn off cloud syncing</strong> in the game. To
          delete your backup, see <a href="#delete-your-data">Delete your Shelly Jigsaw data</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="icloud">Cloud save with iCloud (iPhone)</h2>
        <p>
          On iPhone, Shelly Jigsaw can back up your progress with <strong>iCloud</strong>, using the
          Apple Account you are signed in with on the iPhone. The game has no sign-in of its own.
          Cloud saving starts when you choose <strong>Settings &gt; Cloud save &gt; Turn on cloud
          saving</strong> in the game.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            A copy of your game progress (puzzle progress, finished puzzles, stars and shells,
            Shelly's field notes and game settings) is saved in the app's private iCloud storage in
            your own Apple Account. Only Shelly Jigsaw can read it, and only for your account. It does
            not include your photos, contacts, or the ad counters. It uses a little of your iCloud
            storage.
          </li>
          <li>
            The game receives an iCloud user code that Apple makes for this app only, so it can make
            sure a backup is restored to the same account. It does not receive your name, email
            address or Apple Account details, and we do not keep the code anywhere except on your
            phone.
          </li>
          <li>
            When you install the game on a new iPhone signed in to the same Apple Account, or
            reinstall it, your saved progress is restored. If both your phone and your backup have
            progress, the game asks which one to keep.
          </li>
          <li>
            iCloud is run by Apple under the
            <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/" rel="noopener">Apple Privacy Policy</a>.
            The backup is sent to Apple over an encrypted connection, not to us, and we cannot open it.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <strong>Your choices.</strong> You can turn cloud saving off at any time with
          <strong>Settings &gt; Cloud save &gt; Turn off cloud syncing</strong> in the game. To
          delete your backup, see <a href="#delete-your-data">Delete your Shelly Jigsaw data</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="game-measurement">How we measure the game</h2>
        <p>
          Shelly Jigsaw uses <strong>Google Analytics for Firebase</strong>, a Google service, to
          understand which puzzles people enjoy and to improve the game and its ads. The app sends
          Google game events, such as when a puzzle is started, finished, shared or left, how long it took,
          hints used and video ads watched. These events never include your name, email or photos.
        </p>
        <p>
          Google Analytics also records basic app information, such as when the app was first
          opened, how long it is used, the app version, the phone model, the Android or iOS version,
          the country, and a random app-instance identifier. On Android, when ads are allowed, it can
          use the advertising ID to measure which ads brought players to the game. On iPhone it never
          uses Apple's advertising identifier (IDFA). We look at these numbers as totals and cannot
          tell who you are.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the European Economic Area, the UK and Switzerland, your choice in the consent message
          shown at first launch also applies to this measurement. Google keeps the detailed
          measurement data for 14 months, and then only totals remain. Learn more in
          <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites" rel="noopener">how Google uses information from apps that use its services</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="meta">Measuring our ads with Meta (Android)</h2>
        <p>
          We advertise Shelly Jigsaw on Facebook and Instagram. The Android app includes the
          <strong>Meta SDK</strong>, a tool from Meta Platforms, so Meta can tell whether its ads led
          to the game being installed and played, and show the ads to people more likely to enjoy
          the game. The iPhone app does not include it.
        </p>
        <p>When the Meta SDK runs, the app sends Meta:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>that the app was installed or opened, and that you finished your first puzzle;</li>
          <li>your device's advertising ID and an anonymous identifier the SDK creates;</li>
          <li>your IP address, which can indicate your approximate location;</li>
          <li>
            information about your device, such as model, Android version, language, time zone,
            screen size and free storage space;
          </li>
          <li>the app's name and version, and crash reports about the Meta SDK itself.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          It never sends your name, email or photos, or any other game events. Meta may connect this
          information with a Facebook or Instagram account you have, and uses it under the
          <a href="https://www.facebook.com/privacy/policy/" rel="noopener">Meta Privacy Policy</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Meta SDK does not run if you are in the European Economic Area, the UK, Switzerland, or
          a US state where the app offers Privacy choices. You can delete your advertising ID in your
          phone's settings, as described below. If you have a Facebook or Instagram account, you can
          also manage this in its settings, under your activity off Meta technologies.
        </p>

        <h2 id="delete-your-data">Delete your Shelly Jigsaw data</h2>
        <p>
          Ohana Studios does not keep any of your Shelly Jigsaw data on its own servers, so you can
          delete everything yourself:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Progress on your phone:</strong> uninstall Shelly Jigsaw, or open Android
            <em>Settings &gt; Apps &gt; Shelly Jigsaw &gt; Storage &gt; Clear data</em>. This deletes
            your puzzle progress, finished puzzles, stars and shells, field notes, settings and ad
            counters from the phone right away. On iPhone, touch and hold the Shelly Jigsaw icon,
            then choose <em>Remove App &gt; Delete App</em>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Cloud backup:</strong> on a computer, open
            <a href="https://drive.google.com" rel="noopener">Google Drive</a>, choose
            <em>Settings &gt; Manage apps</em>, find <strong>Shelly Jigsaw</strong>, then choose
            <em>Options &gt; Delete hidden app data</em>. This deletes the backup copy of your
            progress from your Google account. To stop new backups, first turn off
            <strong>Settings &gt; Cloud save</strong> in the game, or uninstall it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>iCloud backup (iPhone):</strong> first turn off <strong>Settings &gt; Cloud
            save</strong> in the game. Then open iPhone <em>Settings</em>, tap your name, then
            <em>iCloud &gt; Manage Account Storage</em> (or <em>Storage</em>), choose
            <strong>Shelly Jigsaw</strong> and <em>Delete Data</em>. If Shelly Jigsaw is not
            listed there, email us and we will help.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Game measurement data:</strong> it is not linked to your name or Google
            account. Uninstalling the app or clearing its data resets the app-instance identifier,
            and Google deletes the detailed measurement data after 14 months. To ask us to delete the
            data for your installation sooner, email us.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Google Play Games profile data:</strong> you can delete the Play Games data for
            this game, or your whole Play Games profile, in the Google Play Games app or in your
            <a href="https://myaccount.google.com" rel="noopener">Google Account</a>.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          <strong>What is kept:</strong> nothing is kept by Ohana Studios. Information collected by
          Google for ads, game measurement and Google Play Games is handled under Google's own retention policies;
          you can manage ad data at <a href="https://myadcenter.google.com" rel="noopener">My Ad Center</a>.
          Information sent to Meta is handled under Meta's own retention policies.
          If you need help, email us at <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Ads from Google AdMob</h2>
        <p>
          Shelly Jigsaw shows three kinds of ads: optional videos you can choose to watch for an extra
          hint or to double the shells you earn for a puzzle, occasional full-screen ads between
          puzzles, and a small banner below the puzzle. Ads never cover the pieces, and full-screen
          ads never interrupt a puzzle you are solving.
        </p>
        <p>
          The ads are provided by Google AdMob. When the app requests or shows an ad, Google may
          collect and process:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            device identifiers: on Android, your device's advertising ID and app set ID; on iPhone,
            the identifier for vendors (IDFV) and, only if you allow tracking, the identifier for
            advertisers (IDFA);
          </li>
          <li>your IP address, which can indicate your approximate location;</li>
          <li>information about your device, such as model, operating system and language;</li>
          <li>how you interact with ads, such as views and taps;</li>
          <li>diagnostic information, such as ad loading errors.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Google uses this information to deliver ads, limit how often you see the same ad,
          measure ad performance, prevent fraud and, where you allow it, show more relevant ads.
          This information is sent to Google, not to us. We only see anonymous, combined reports
          (for example, how many ads were shown). Google's use of this information is described in
          <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites" rel="noopener">How Google uses information from sites or apps that use its services</a>
          and the <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener">Google Privacy Policy</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Your choices about ads</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Consent in the EEA, UK and Switzerland.</strong> If you are in these regions, the
            app asks for your consent through Google's consent message before personalized ads are
            shown. You can change your choice at any time with <strong>Privacy choices</strong> in
            the game's Settings.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Tracking on iPhone.</strong> The game asks once, with Apple's message, whether it
            may track your activity across other companies' apps and websites. If you choose
            <strong>Ask App Not to Track</strong>, Google AdMob cannot use your IDFA and ads are
            less tailored. You can change this at any time in iPhone
            <em>Settings &gt; Privacy &amp; Security &gt; Tracking</em>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Advertising ID on Android.</strong> You can reset or delete your advertising ID,
            or opt out of personalized ads, in your phone's settings (usually
            <em>Settings &gt; Google &gt; Ads</em> or <em>Settings &gt; Privacy &gt; Ads</em>).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Personalized ads in general.</strong> You can manage Google ad personalization at
            <a href="https://myadcenter.google.com" rel="noopener">My Ad Center</a>.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>If you opt out of personalized ads, you will still see ads, but they will be less tailored.</p>

        <h2>Phone permissions</h2>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Permission</th><th>Why the app uses it</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Internet and network state</td><td>To load ads, to back up your progress with Google Play Games or iCloud, and to send the game measurements described above. Puzzles are built into the app and do not need a connection.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Advertising ID</td><td>Used by Google AdMob, and on Android by Google Analytics and the Meta SDK to measure ads, as described above.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Tracking (iPhone)</td><td>Asked with Apple's tracking message. If you allow it, Google AdMob can use the IDFA to show more relevant ads. The game works the same either way.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Ad and install measurement (install referrer, Android ad services)</td><td>Used by Google AdMob, Google Analytics and the Meta SDK to measure which ads and store pages bring players to the game and, where you allow it, to show more relevant ads.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Background work (Android: wake lock, foreground service)</td><td>Used by Google's ads and measurement code to finish sending its data when the app is closed. The game itself only schedules the Daily reminder, if you turn it on.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Notifications (Android)</td><td>Asked only if you tap Remind me after finishing a Daily. The app then sends at most one quiet note a day, only on days you have not opened the game, and stops after three in a row. You can turn it off any time in Android's notification settings.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Vibration</td><td>For the gentle buzz when pieces fit. You can turn it off in Settings.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Photos, add only (iPhone)</td><td>Used only if you tap Save Image in the phone's share sheet to save a framed puzzle picture to Photos. The app never reads your photo library.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          The app does not read your photo library, contacts, camera, microphone or precise
          location. It only receives a photo you pick yourself in your phone's photo picker, as
          described in <a href="#your-own-photos">Your own photos</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Children</h2>
        <p>
          Shelly Jigsaw is made for adults and is not directed at children under 13. We do not
          knowingly collect personal information from children. If you believe a child has provided
          personal information, please contact us and we will help.
        </p>

        <h2>Your rights</h2>
        <p>
          Depending on where you live (for example under the GDPR in Europe or state laws in the
          United States), you may have the right to access, correct or delete personal information,
          or to opt out of its "sale" or "sharing" for targeted advertising. Because we do not hold
          personal information about you, most requests are best handled by the choices above or
          directly with Google, which holds your ad data and your Android cloud backup, or with Apple for your iCloud backup. We are happy to help with any request: just email us.
        </p>

        <h2>Security and retention</h2>
        <p>
          We do not run servers that store your game data. Your progress is kept on your phone and,
          if you use cloud saving, in your own Google account or iCloud until you delete it as
          described above. Information collected by Google for ads, game measurement and Google Play Games is kept
          according to Google's own retention policies.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
        <p>
          If we change how the app uses information, we will update this page and its effective
          date. Important changes will also be mentioned in the app's store listing.
        </p>

        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>
          {STUDIO}<br />
          Email: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
